From 1e7d49dec592227706b333d58ffb36fe2f922853 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Liu Dongmiao <473522+liudongmiao@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 11:39:14 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 10/12] quic: do not send a PING in reply to a received PING

A received PING called ossl_quic_tx_packetiser_schedule_ack_eliciting(),
which forced the next packet to be ack-eliciting, so the reply carried a
PING of its own. For a server, a PING in a client Initial packet thus
led to an ack-eliciting Initial packet padded to 1200 bytes. It cost
anti-amplification credit and restarted the Initial PTO timer, which
delayed resending a lost ServerHello. The call also passed an encryption
level as a packet number space, so a PING in a Handshake packet forced a
PING in the next 1-RTT packet.

The receiver of a PING only needs to acknowledge it (RFC 9000 s. 19.2),
and the packet carrying it is already ack-eliciting, so drop the call.

Add a quicfaultstest test that a PING in a server Handshake packet does
not make the client send a PING.

