From d25e4aac76182dd70f83c9daed51fe3cc2d196de Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Liu Dongmiao <473522+liudongmiao@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 18:28:54 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 12/12] quic: probe all spaces with data in flight, and on
 client packets

On a PTO, only the packet number space whose timer expired was probed. A
server with a lost ServerHello and Handshake data in flight then sent
Handshake probes, which the client cannot decrypt without the
ServerHello, while the ServerHello waited for an Initial PTO. Also probe
the other spaces with ack-eliciting packets in flight, as RFC 9002
s. 6.2.4 recommends, so that the probes are coalesced into one datagram.

A server also resent its data only on a PTO, whose interval doubles each
time. Under heavy loss the client often gave up before a resend arrived,
although it kept sending Initial or Handshake packets that the server
only acknowledged. When an ack-eliciting Initial or Handshake packet
arrives from a client that has not acknowledged all of the server's data
in that space, request a probe in that space at once, so the data is
resent in reply. Do this at most twice per space and not while a probe
is already pending. RFC 9002 s. 6.2.3 allows sending unacknowledged
CRYPTO data before the PTO a limited number of times per connection.

Add a quic_txp_test script for a PTO with Initial and Handshake CRYPTO
data in flight, and quic_ackm_test tests for the early probes in both
spaces, including the limit, a packet that arrives while a probe is
pending, and the case where the data is already acknowledged.

